<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#gilchrist-thomas-process">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gilchrist–Thomas process</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Thomas process, basic Bessemer process</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gilchrist–Thomas process is a method of making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> from phosphorus-rich iron ore, developed by the British cousins Sidney Gilchrist Thomas and Percy Gilchrist and made public in 1879. By lining the Bessemer converter with a basic material such as dolomite, it removed phosphorus from the molten iron. It made the phosphoric minette ores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and Luxembourg usable and drove the rise of heavy industry there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gilchrist-thomas-process.jpg" alt="Gilchrist–Thomas process" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rainer Halama · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phoenixsee89124.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
